<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Punic War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/218-bce">218</a>–201 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Punic War was fought from 218 to 201 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and won great victories such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>, but could not force Rome to surrender, while Roman forces conquered Carthaginian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> defeated Hannibal at Zama, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> treat the war as the end of Carthage as a great power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-punic-war.jpg" alt="Second Punic War" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Robert Shepherd · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Rome_and_Carthage_at_the_start_of_the_Second_Punic_War.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Numidia, united by Masinissa after the <b>Second Punic War</b>, was the first great Berber kingdom, spreading Punic urban culture inland, helping Rome destroy Carthage and then, under Jugurtha, fighting a war that exposed the corruption of the Roman Republic before its absorption into Roman Africa in 46 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire’s supreme military achievement was Hannibal Barca’s invasion of Italy during the <b>Second Punic War</b>, beginning with his audacious crossing of the Alps in 218 BCE with some 26,000 surviving troops and a dwindling elephant corps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Second Punic War</b> Hannibal destroyed Roman armies at the Trebia, Lake Trasimene and Cannae in 218–216 BCE, but Rome refused terms, and Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus defeated him at the Battle of Zama in 202 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Punic of Carthage developed somewhat different, more cursive letter shapes, and around the time of the <b>Second Punic War</b> an even more cursive form, Neo-Punic, appeared beside the conservative one and became predominant some time after the destruction of Carthage in about 149 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamilcar Barca, then Hasdrubal the Fair, founder of Qart Hadasht (Cartagena), and from 221 BCE Hannibal conquered the Iberians south of the Ebro, and Hannibal’s siege of Saguntum, an ally of Rome, began the <b>Second Punic War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>At least eighty-five dictators were appointed before the office lapsed after the <b>Second Punic War</b>, among them Quintus Fabius Maximus, whose delaying tactics against Hannibal in 217 BCE earned him the name Cunctator, “the Delayer”.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
